  "body": "Section §3.3 famously modifies the rules for XPath 1.0 in the case where \"the context node is from an HTML DOM\"\r\n\r\nIs this rule intended to apply where the context node for the XPath expression as a whole is from an HTML DOM, or does it apply where the context node for an individual axis step is from an HTML DOM?\r\n\r\nFor XPath 1.0 the question might be considered a little academic, since it's quite hard to navigate outside a single DOM tree using pure XPath 1.0 features. But it can be done if external binding of variables is allowed, e.g. `//table//tr[td[1]=$param/author/name]/td[2]`. The question here is, how is the unprefixed name `author`, which is presumably evaluated within an XML DOM, to be interpreted? \r\n\r\nFor later XPath versions mixing of multiple documents becomes perfectly normal, so it would be good to know what the WG had in mind. As written, the rule is quite ambiguous.",
